export const INSIGHT_HTML: Record<string, string> = {
  "developer-builder-vs-construct-only-builder": [
    "<p>Search for industrial warehouse builders in Brisbane and most of the results are construction companies that will price a finished set of drawings. That suits you if you already have a design, a development approval and someone to run a tender. It is less useful if you own industrial land and need a team to take it from an idea to a building you can occupy, lease or sell.</p>",
    "<p>Brenscot is a developer-builder. We design, develop and build industrial warehouses across South East Queensland, from single freestanding facilities to multi-unit complexes. This article explains what that means, how it differs from a construct-only builder, and how to work out which one your project needs.</p>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>A construct-only builder builds what someone else has designed and had approved. A developer-builder takes responsibility for more of the journey: assessing the site, designing a building that can be approved and built efficiently, managing the approvals, constructing it and handing over a finished asset. You deal with one team, and that team is accountable for how the design, the approvals and the construction fit together.</p>",
    "<h2>What a construct-only builder does</h2>",
    "<p>Under a construct-only contract, you (or your architect and project manager) prepare the design, obtain the approvals and call tenders. The builder prices the documents and builds to them. The builder is responsible for workmanship and for building what is drawn. The builder is generally not responsible for whether the design is complete, efficient or suited to your site, because the builder did not produce it.</p>",
    "<p>This model works well when the design is finished and well documented. It works poorly when the documents are incomplete, because every gap becomes a variation during construction.</p>",
    "<h2>What a developer-builder does</h2>",
    "<h3>1. Assesses the site before anything is drawn</h3>",
    "<p>A warehouse is shaped by its site. Before design starts we look at the things that decide what can be built and what it will cost:</p>",
    "<ul><li>Zoning and overlays under the local planning scheme, such as flood, acid sulfate soils and bushfire</li><li>Ground conditions, through a geotechnical investigation</li><li>Site levels, stormwater discharge and the services available (power, water, sewer, communications)</li><li>Truck access, turning paths and the hardstand depth the intended vehicles need</li><li>How much building the site can carry once setbacks, site cover, parking, landscaping and stormwater treatment are allowed for</li></ul>",
    "<p>On our own developments, Indevelop (our development arm) identifies and acquires the land. On client-owned land, we start from your title and your brief.</p>",
    "<h3>2. Designs a building that can be approved and built</h3>",
    "<p>Warehouse design is driven by operations and by regulation, not by the elevation. Clear height, door sizes, office size, the wall system, fire compartment size under the National Construction Code (NCC), and truck circulation all affect each other. Because we design as the team that will also build, the detailing stays tied to programme, cost and buildability from the first sketch.</p>",
    "<p>Related reading: NCC construction Types A, B and C and warehouse wall systems compared.</p>",
    "<h3>3. Manages the approvals</h3>",
    "<p>In Queensland, most new warehouses pass through two separate approval steps:</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Development approval</strong> under the Planning Act 2016, assessed by the local council against its planning scheme. Whether it is needed, and whether it is code or impact assessable, depends on the zone, the use and the site.</li><li><strong>Building approval</strong> under the Building Act 1975, assessed by a building certifier (almost always a private certifier) against the NCC and Queensland's building assessment provisions.</li></ul>",
    "<p>Each SEQ council (Brisbane, Moreton Bay, Logan, Ipswich, Gold Coast and others) has its own planning scheme and its own requirements. We coordinate the town planner, engineers, certifier and other consultants and manage the applications. We do not promise approval timeframes, because councils and referral agencies control their own assessment. See how long a warehouse build takes.</p>",
    "<h3>4. Builds it, with the critical works under direct control</h3>",
    "<p>We hold a QBCC open builder licence and manage construction ourselves. We work with a select group of proven subcontractors and consultants, and we keep the works that most affect programme and quality under our direct control.</p>",
    "<h3>5. Hands over a finished asset</h3>",
    "<p>For an owner-occupier, that is a building designed around how the business runs, with a certificate of occupancy. For an investor, it is a building designed to lease or sell. On Indevelop sites, the completed buildings are offered for sale or lease. See our <a href=\"/developments\">projects</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>Developer-builder vs construct-only builder</h2>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Your situation</th><th scope=\"col\">Construct-only builder</th><th scope=\"col\">Developer-builder (Brenscot)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Complete design and approvals in hand, want competitive prices</td><td>Good fit</td><td>Not our main service</td></tr><tr><td>Own land, need design, approvals and construction</td><td>You manage several separate parties</td><td>One team from concept to handover</td></tr><tr><td>Want a finished building to buy or lease</td><td>Not offered</td><td>Available through Indevelop developments</td></tr><tr><td>Who is responsible for design gaps</td><td>You and your designers</td><td>The developer-builder, under a design and construct contract</td></tr><tr><td>When the builder's input reaches the design</td><td>After the design is finished</td><td>From the first concept</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<p>Neither model is better in every case. They solve different problems. The common mistake is choosing a construct-only tender when the design and approvals are not actually finished. More on this in <a href=\"/insights/design-and-construct-vs-tender\">design and construct vs tender</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>Who this model suits</h2>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Landowners</strong> with industrial-zoned land who want a complete building delivered without managing a dozen separate contracts</li><li><strong>Owner-occupiers</strong> who need a warehouse designed around their vehicles, racking, plant and staff</li><li><strong>Investors</strong> who want freestanding or multi-unit industrial product designed to lease or sell</li><li><strong>Commercial agents</strong> with clients looking for a new building, or land vendors looking for a delivery partner. See <a href=\"/for-commercial-agents\">for commercial agents</a>.</li></ul>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>What is a developer-builder?</h3>",
    "<p>A developer-builder is a company that both develops property and builds it. It can acquire land, design the building, obtain approvals, construct the building and then sell or lease it. It can also deliver the same design, approvals and construction service on land a client already owns.</p>",
    "<h3>What is the difference between a developer and a builder?</h3>",
    "<p>A developer takes the commercial risk on a project: buying the land, funding it and finding the buyer or tenant. A builder is contracted to construct the building. A developer-builder does both, which removes the handover between the two.</p>",
    "<h3>Do I need to own land to work with Brenscot?</h3>",
    "<p>No. If you own land, we deliver design and construction on it. If you don't, buildings on our own development sites are available to purchase or lease.</p>",
    "<h3>Is a developer-builder more expensive than tendering to several builders?</h3>",
    "<p>Not necessarily. A tender compares prices for the documents issued. If those documents are incomplete, the lowest tender price is not the final cost. A design and construct price covers both the design and the construction, so the comparison needs to be made on the finished building, not the tender figure.</p>",
    "<h3>What licence should an industrial builder hold in Queensland?</h3>",
    "<p>In Queensland, a company carrying out building work valued at more than $3,300 must hold a Queensland Building and Construction Commission (QBCC) licence. A \"Builder – Open\" licence covers building work on all classes of building, with specialist work such as fire protection and plumbing done by separately licensed contractors. You can check any licence on the QBCC licensee register. Brenscot Builders Pty Ltd holds QBCC licence 15213515.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>If you have industrial land, or need a warehouse and want to understand your options, call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au. You can also read about <a href=\"/process\">our process</a> or go to <a href=\"/industrial-builders-brisbane\">industrial builders Brisbane</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>Planning Act 2016 (Qld), sections 44 and 45: categories of development and assessment</li><li>Building Act 1975 (Qld), section 48: functions of private certifiers</li><li>Queensland Building and Construction Commission: licensee register and licence classes, qbcc.qld.gov.au</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information, current as at September 2026. It is not legal, planning, financial or design advice. Requirements differ between sites and councils. Confirm the requirements for your project with your town planner, building certifier and engineers.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "design-and-construct-vs-tender": [
    "<p>There are two common ways to procure a new warehouse. Under design and construct (D&amp;C), one company designs the building and builds it. Under a traditional tender, you have the building designed first, then ask several builders to price the finished drawings and build to them.</p>",
    "<p>Both end with a warehouse. They differ in who is responsible for the design, when the builder gets involved, and where the risk of gaps and changes sits. The right choice depends on how finished your brief and your drawings really are.</p>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>Choose a traditional tender when the design is complete, coordinated and approved, and you want builders to compete on price. Choose design and construct when you have land and a brief but not a finished design, and you want one company responsible for getting from concept to a completed building. Most problems arise when a project is tendered before the design is genuinely finished.</p>",
    "<h2>How design and construct works</h2>",
    "<p>You give the builder a brief: what the building needs to do, the site, the budget and the timing. The builder engages and coordinates the designers and consultants, develops the design, obtains the approvals and constructs the building under a single contract.</p>",
    "<p>The consultants still do their jobs and still certify their own work. A structural engineer designs the structure, a civil engineer designs the earthworks and stormwater, and a private building certifier assesses the building against the National Construction Code (NCC). The difference is that they are coordinated by the company that has to build the result, and that company carries the responsibility for the design being buildable and complete.</p>",
    "<p>What D&amp;C does not do is remove all risk. Genuine changes to the brief, latent ground conditions (hidden problems such as rock or soft soil) and authority requirements can still change cost and time. What it reduces is the gap between what was drawn and what can actually be built for the money.</p>",
    "<h2>How a traditional tender works</h2>",
    "<p>You engage an architect or building designer and the engineers directly. They complete the design and documentation, and you obtain the approvals. You then invite builders to tender, compare the prices and award a construct-only contract.</p>",
    "<p>This works when four things are true:</p>",
    "<ol><li>The brief is settled and will not change during construction</li><li>The drawings and specifications are complete enough that every builder is pricing the same thing</li><li>The structural, civil, fire and services designs are coordinated with each other</li><li>Someone on your side is managing the consultants and the builder, and resolving questions as they arise</li></ol>",
    "<p>When those conditions are not met, the tender price is not the final price. Each omission or clash in the documents becomes a variation, and the builder is generally entitled to be paid for it because the builder did not produce the design.</p>",
    "<h2>Side by side</h2>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Question</th><th scope=\"col\">Design and construct</th><th scope=\"col\">Traditional tender (construct only)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who is responsible for the design?</td><td>The builder, under one contract</td><td>You and your consultants</td></tr><tr><td>When does the builder get involved?</td><td>From concept</td><td>After the design is finished</td></tr><tr><td>Best suited to</td><td>A brief and a site, with the design still to be developed</td><td>A complete, coordinated and approved design</td></tr><tr><td>Main cause of variations</td><td>Changes to the brief</td><td>Gaps in the documents, plus changes to the brief</td></tr><tr><td>Price competition</td><td>On the brief, between D&amp;C builders</td><td>On the drawings, between tenderers</td></tr><tr><td>Number of contracts you manage</td><td>One</td><td>Several: designers, consultants and builder</td></tr><tr><td>Programme</td><td>Design, approvals and procurement can overlap</td><td>Sequential: design, then tender, then build</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<h2>Why D&amp;C suits most SEQ warehouse projects</h2>",
    "<p>A warehouse is a simple-looking building with a lot of interdependent decisions. A few examples:</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Height and fire compartment size.</strong> Under NCC 2022 Volume One, Table C3D3, a Type C warehouse fire compartment is limited to 2,000 m2 of floor area and 12,000 m3 of volume. Adding a metre of clear height can push a building over the volume limit and trigger fire walls or other measures. See fire walls and fire compartments.</li><li><strong>Site levels and stormwater.</strong> The stormwater design sets the surface levels, which set the slab level, the driveway grades and the retaining walls. If the civil design is finalised after the building design, something has to be redrawn.</li><li><strong>Wall system and boundary setbacks.</strong> In a typical (Type C) warehouse, an external wall less than 3 m from a side or rear boundary needs a fire resistance level under the NCC. That affects whether the wall can be metal cladding or needs to be concrete. See wall systems compared.</li></ul>",
    "<p>When these decisions are made by separate consultants at different times, they conflict. When they are made together by the team that will build the result, they can be resolved early, when changes cost the least.</p>",
    "<h2>When a tender still makes sense</h2>",
    "<p>If you already have full engineering, a resolved fire and civil design, development and building approvals, and someone to manage the contract, tendering to several builders is a legitimate strategy. It is not the service Brenscot focuses on, and we would rather say so than price documents we had no part in.</p>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>What is a design and construct contract?</h3>",
    "<p>It is a building contract under which the builder is responsible for both designing and constructing the building to meet the client's brief. In Australia, standard forms such as AS 4902 are commonly used for D&amp;C, although many projects use amended or bespoke contracts. Have any contract reviewed by a construction lawyer.</p>",
    "<h3>Who carries the design risk under design and construct?</h3>",
    "<p>Primarily the builder, subject to the terms of the contract. Under construct only, the client carries the risk that the design is incomplete or wrong, because the client supplied it.</p>",
    "<h3>Is design and construct cheaper than tendering?</h3>",
    "<p>It can be, but compare total finished cost, not the contract sum alone. A tender price covers only what is documented. A D&amp;C price covers the design fees, the approvals management and the construction.</p>",
    "<h3>Can I still choose my own finishes and layout under D&amp;C?</h3>",
    "<p>Yes. You set the brief and approve the design as it develops. The earlier a decision is made, the less it costs to change.</p>",
    "<h3>Does Brenscot tender on other people's drawings?</h3>",
    "<p>Our focus is design and construct and our own developments. If your design is substantially complete we are happy to discuss it, but we will tell you if we think a tender is the better path.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>To talk through a site or a brief, call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au. Read more about <a href=\"/design-and-construct-warehouse-brisbane\">design and construct warehouses in Brisbane</a> and <a href=\"/process\">our process</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>National Construction Code 2022, Volume One, Table C3D3 (maximum size of fire compartments) and Specification 5 (fire-resisting construction), Australian Building Codes Board, ncc.abcb.gov.au</li><li>Standards Australia, AS 4902 General conditions of contract for design and construct</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information, current as at September 2026. It is not legal, procurement or design advice. Contract forms and risk allocation are specific to each project. Obtain advice from a construction lawyer before signing a building contract.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "turnkey-industrial-development-brisbane": [
    "<p>\"Turnkey\" is used loosely in construction. For us it has a specific meaning: one team takes a site from an idea to a finished industrial building that is approved, certified and ready to use. You are not left to coordinate a planner, an architect, five engineers, a certifier and a builder under separate contracts.</p>",
    "<p>This article explains the two ways Brenscot delivers turnkey industrial buildings in South East Queensland, and what owner-occupiers and investors should each decide early.</p>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>If you own industrial land, we design the building, obtain the approvals and construct it to your brief. If you don't own land, Indevelop (our development arm) develops sites of its own, and the completed buildings are available to buy or lease. In both cases Brenscot manages design, approvals and construction from start to finish.</p>",
    "<h2>Two ways to get a turnkey building</h2>",
    "<h3>Turnkey delivery on your land</h3>",
    "<p>You own or control the site. We assess it, design the building to your brief, manage the development and building approvals, construct it and hand it over with a certificate of occupancy. This suits owner-occupiers, and investors who hold land but do not want to manage a group of consultants and a tender.</p>",
    "<p>More detail: <a href=\"/design-and-construct-warehouse-brisbane\">design and construct warehouse Brisbane</a>.</p>",
    "<h3>Build to suit on our sites</h3>",
    "<p>Indevelop identifies and acquires industrial land. Brenscot designs and builds on it, and the completed buildings are offered for sale or lease. Where timing allows, a building can be tailored to an occupier's requirements before or during construction. This suits occupiers and investors who want a new building without buying land and running a development.</p>",
    "<p>See current and completed <a href=\"/developments\">projects</a>. Commercial agents can read <a href=\"/for-commercial-agents\">how we work with agents</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>Owner-occupiers: design around the business</h2>",
    "<p>A warehouse that is briefed as \"a shed with an office\" usually ends up costing the business in daily inefficiency. Four things deserve early decisions.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Vehicles and loading.</strong> Which trucks will use the site: rigid trucks, semi-trailers or B-doubles? Will they reverse to a dock, drive through or be unloaded by forklift at ground level? This sets the hardstand depth, gate position and door locations. See clear height, doors and truck access.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Height and storage.</strong> Clear height sets what racking you can fit. It also sets the building's volume, which the National Construction Code (NCC) limits for each fire compartment. How you store goods matters too: under NCC 2022 clause E1D13, a fire compartment larger than 2,000 m2 or 12,000 m3 needs sprinklers if it holds more than 1,000 m3 of combustible goods stored higher than 4 m. See <a href=\"/insights/warehouse-fire-services-hydrants-sprinklers\">fire services for warehouses</a>.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Office and amenities.</strong> Size the office for how the business actually operates. Air-conditioned office space also carries NCC insulation and glazing requirements that an unconditioned warehouse generally does not. Lighting and energy metering rules still apply to the warehouse. See <a href=\"/insights/warehouse-insulation-condensation-section-j\">insulation, condensation and Section J</a>.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Power and future needs.</strong> Three-phase supply capacity, solar, electric vehicle charging, gantry cranes and future expansion are all far cheaper to allow for in design than to retrofit.</p>",
    "<h2>Investors: design a building that leases and sells</h2>",
    "<p>Investors need a building that tenants want on day one and that holds its value. The first decision is the product type, because freestanding and multi-unit buildings are designed differently.</p>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"></th><th scope=\"col\">Freestanding building</th><th scope=\"col\">Multi-unit complex</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Designed around</td><td>One occupier's requirements</td><td>A mix of unit sizes with shared access and parking</td></tr><tr><td>Fire separation</td><td>Usually one fire compartment, subject to NCC size limits</td><td>Fire walls between units, usually on the lot boundaries</td></tr><tr><td>Title</td><td>Usually a single lot on one title</td><td>Usually separate lots in a community titles scheme with a body corporate</td></tr><tr><td>Exit</td><td>Sale to an occupier, or a leased investment</td><td>Individual unit sales, or a leased portfolio</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<p>Multi-unit projects also need the titling designed in from the start. The unit boundaries, fire walls, services metering and exclusive-use yards all need to match the future survey plan and community management statement. See community titles and plan sealing for industrial units.</p>",
    "<h2>What \"turnkey\" covers, step by step</h2>",
    "<ol><li><strong>Site assessment</strong>: planning scheme, overlays, survey, geotechnical investigation, services and access</li><li><strong>Concept design and budget</strong>: building size, height, wall system, fire strategy and civil concept developed together</li><li><strong>Development approval</strong>, where the planning scheme requires it</li><li><strong>Detailed design and building approval</strong> through a private building certifier</li><li><strong>Construction</strong>, with inspections at the stages nominated by the certifier</li><li><strong>Completion</strong>: certificate of occupancy, as-constructed documents, and plan sealing and titles for multi-unit projects</li></ol>",
    "<p>Our <a href=\"/process\">process</a> page sets this out in more detail. For the approvals and certification steps, see the building certification pathway in Queensland.</p>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>What does turnkey mean in construction?</h3>",
    "<p>It means the builder delivers a completed building ready to use, having managed the design, approvals and construction. The client provides the brief and makes decisions, but does not coordinate the individual consultants and trades.</p>",
    "<h3>Can I get a new warehouse without owning land?</h3>",
    "<p>Yes. Buildings on Indevelop development sites are offered for sale or lease, and in some cases can be adapted to an occupier's needs during design or construction.</p>",
    "<h3>Is a freestanding warehouse or a multi-unit complex the better investment?</h3>",
    "<p>They carry different risks. A freestanding building depends on one tenant or buyer. A multi-unit complex spreads that across several smaller occupiers but has more design, titling and body corporate complexity. Speak with your financial adviser and a commercial agent active in the area.</p>",
    "<h3>Which parts of South East Queensland does Brenscot work in?</h3>",
    "<p>We work across South East Queensland, with most of our projects on Brisbane's northside and in the Moreton Bay region.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>If you are weighing up land, a build-to-suit or multi-unit stock, call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>National Construction Code 2022, Volume One: Table C3D3 (fire compartment size), clause E1D13 (occupancies of excessive hazard), Section J (energy efficiency), Australian Building Codes Board, ncc.abcb.gov.au</li><li>Body Corporate and Community Management Act 1997 (Qld)</li><li>Building Act 1975 (Qld), sections 102 and 114: certificate of occupancy</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information, current as at September 2026. It is not financial, legal, planning or design advice, and investment outcomes are not guaranteed. Confirm the requirements for your project with the relevant advisers.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "warehouse-construction-cost-drivers-brisbane": [
    "<p>The first question most people ask is \"what's the rate per square metre?\". It is a reasonable question with an unhelpful answer, because two warehouses of the same floor area on different sites, with different heights, walls, hardstand and fire services, can differ in cost by a wide margin.</p>",
    "<p>We don't publish rates. Construction costs are still moving quickly and any figure we printed would soon be wrong. What we can do is explain what drives the cost, so you can make informed decisions early and compare prices properly.</p>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>The building itself is only part of the cost. Ground conditions, site levels, hardstand area, stormwater treatment, fire services, services connections and government charges can together rival the cost of the structure. Most of these are set by the site and by early design decisions, particularly building height, floor area and wall system. The cheapest time to reduce cost is before the design is fixed.</p>",
    "<h2>What does a \"rate per m2\" include?</h2>",
    "<p>Published and quoted rates use different measures. Some are per m2 of gross floor area, some per m2 of warehouse floor only. More importantly, they include different things. Rates published by quantity surveyors typically cover the building only and exclude items such as site works and drainage, professional fees, tenancy fitout, land and GST. Always check the exclusions list.</p>",
    "<p>When you compare prices, separate the cost into these groups and check what each price includes:</p>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Cost group</th><th scope=\"col\">What it covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Building</td><td>Slab, structure, walls, roof, doors, base office and amenities</td></tr><tr><td>Site and external works</td><td>Earthworks, retaining walls, hardstand, car parking, crossovers, fencing, landscaping, stormwater drainage and treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Services</td><td>Power supply and any transformer or network upgrade, water, sewer, fire services, communications</td></tr><tr><td>Design and approvals</td><td>Consultants, certifier, application fees</td></tr><tr><td>Government charges and levies</td><td>Infrastructure charges, QLeave levies, utility connection fees</td></tr><tr><td>Owner's costs</td><td>Land, finance and holding costs, legal fees, fitout, racking and equipment</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<h2>The drivers that move the cost most</h2>",
    "<h3>Ground conditions and foundations</h3>",
    "<p>The geotechnical report determines the foundation system and the slab design. Shallow pad footings on good ground cost far less than piles through soft clay or uncontrolled fill. This is the largest site-specific unknown on most projects, which is why the investigation should happen before the budget is set. See footings and foundations.</p>",
    "<h3>Site levels, earthworks and retaining</h3>",
    "<p>A warehouse needs a large, near-level platform. On a sloping site that means cut and fill, imported or exported material, and <a href=\"/insights/warehouse-retaining-walls-types\">retaining walls</a>. Fill that will support the building must be placed and tested as controlled fill.</p>",
    "<h3>Height and volume</h3>",
    "<p>Every extra metre of clear height adds wall area, heavier structure and higher wind loads. It also adds volume. Under the National Construction Code (NCC) 2022, Table C3D3, a Type C warehouse fire compartment is limited to 2,000 m2 and 12,000 m3. A taller building reaches the volume limit at a smaller floor area, which can trigger fire walls, a higher type of construction or sprinklers. See construction Types A, B and C.</p>",
    "<h3>Wall system</h3>",
    "<p>Full-height concrete tilt panels, concrete dado panels with metal cladding above, and steel framing with metal cladding all have different costs, and the right answer depends partly on how close the walls are to the boundaries. See wall systems compared.</p>",
    "<h3>Slab design</h3>",
    "<p>Slab thickness, reinforcement, concrete strength and joint layout depend on the racking, forklifts and vehicles the floor will carry. A slab designed for high racking with heavy post loads costs more than a general-purpose floor, and is expensive to upgrade later.</p>",
    "<h3>Hardstand and truck access</h3>",
    "<p>The vehicles you need to accommodate set the area and the strength of the pavement. B-double access needs more space and heavier pavement than rigid trucks. Concrete generally costs more than asphalt upfront but is preferred where heavy trucks turn tightly or loads are concentrated. See hardstand options.</p>",
    "<h3>Fire services</h3>",
    "<p>Under NCC 2022 clause E1D2, a building with a total floor area over 500 m2 needs a fire hydrant system where a fire brigade station is within 50 km by road. Whether the town water main can supply the required flow and pressure decides whether you need on-site tanks and pumps. Sprinklers, where required, add capital cost and an ongoing maintenance obligation. See <a href=\"/insights/warehouse-fire-services-hydrants-sprinklers\">fire services</a> and fire walls and compartments.</p>",
    "<h3>Stormwater treatment and detention</h3>",
    "<p>Most new industrial sites need stormwater quality treatment, and many need detention. Bioretention basins take up site area; underground proprietary systems and tanks save space but cost more to install and maintain. See stormwater management.</p>",
    "<h3>Office and amenities</h3>",
    "<p>Air-conditioned office space costs considerably more per square metre than warehouse space. Glazing, ceilings, air conditioning, kitchens, amenities, accessibility requirements and energy efficiency provisions all apply. The size and specification of the office has a larger effect on total cost than most people expect.</p>",
    "<h3>Services connections</h3>",
    "<p>The available power supply is a common surprise. If the business needs more capacity than the street network can provide, the electricity distributor may require a transformer or network upgrade at the developer's cost. Water, sewer and communications connections each carry their own fees.</p>",
    "<h2>Government charges that are often overlooked</h2>",
    "<p><strong>Infrastructure charges.</strong> Councils levy infrastructure charges on new development under the Planning Act 2016, to fund trunk roads, stormwater, parks and (through Urban Utilities or Unitywater) water and sewer networks. The Planning Regulation 2017, Schedule 16, sets the maximum a council can charge. For warehouse and low and medium impact industry uses, the charge has two parts: an amount per m2 of gross floor area, and an amount per m2 of impervious area for stormwater. Each council sets its actual charges in a charges resolution, and they are indexed over time. Credits usually apply for existing lawful uses on the site.</p>",
    "<p><strong>QLeave levies.</strong> In Queensland, combined levies of 0.575% of the cost of work (portable long service leave, work health and safety, and construction training) apply to building and construction work costing $150,000 or more (excluding GST). They are collected by QLeave and must be paid before the development permit for the building work is issued.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Application and connection fees.</strong> Council application fees, certifier fees, Queensland Fire Department referral and inspection fees, and utility connection fees all apply.</p>",
    "<h2>How to keep costs under control</h2>",
    "<ol><li>Commission the survey and geotechnical investigation before setting the budget</li><li>Settle the vehicles, the clear height and the storage method first, because they drive everything else</li><li>Resolve the fire strategy at concept stage, together with height and floor area</li><li>Design the stormwater and site levels alongside the building, not after it</li><li>Compare prices on a like-for-like scope, with the exclusions listed</li><li>Hold a contingency for ground conditions and authority requirements until they are resolved</li></ol>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>How much does it cost to build a warehouse per square metre in Brisbane?</h3>",
    "<p>We don't publish a rate, because costs are changing quickly and the answer depends heavily on the site and specification. Quantity surveying firms publish indicative ranges each year. If you use them, read the exclusions, which commonly include site works, professional fees and GST.</p>",
    "<h3>What is usually not included in a builder's warehouse price?</h3>",
    "<p>Common exclusions are land, finance, infrastructure charges, utility network upgrades, tenant fitout, racking, and latent conditions such as rock or unsuitable fill. Ask every builder for a written list of inclusions and exclusions.</p>",
    "<h3>Is tilt panel more expensive than metal cladding?</h3>",
    "<p>Generally the concrete wall costs more than metal cladding for the same area, but it may remove other costs. A concrete wall can provide the fire resistance needed near a boundary, where a metal-clad wall could not be used without further treatment.</p>",
    "<h3>Does a taller warehouse cost much more?</h3>",
    "<p>Height adds cost directly through walls and structure, and indirectly through fire requirements, because NCC fire compartment limits apply to volume as well as floor area.</p>",
    "<h3>What are infrastructure charges?</h3>",
    "<p>They are charges levied by the council (and the water distributor-retailer) on new development to fund trunk infrastructure. They are calculated mainly on gross floor area and impervious area for industrial uses, and are stated in an infrastructure charges notice issued with the development approval.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>Send us the suburb, approximate building size and a short brief, and we can discuss what will drive the cost on your site. Call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>National Construction Code 2022, Volume One, Table C3D3 and clause E1D2, Australian Building Codes Board, ncc.abcb.gov.au</li><li>Planning Act 2016 (Qld), Chapter 4; Planning Regulation 2017 (Qld), Schedule 16 (prescribed amounts)</li><li>QLeave, building and construction industry levy, qleave.qld.gov.au</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information, current as at September 2026. It is not a quotation or financial advice. Costs depend on the site, the brief, the design and market conditions at the time of pricing.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "warehouse-awnings-fire-compartment-volume": [
    "<p>An awning over the loading area looks like free weather protection. It has no walls, so most people assume it sits outside the building for compliance purposes. Under the National Construction Code (NCC) that is not a safe assumption. Depending on what happens underneath it, an awning can add to the building's measured floor area and volume, and on a warehouse that is already close to its limits, that can change the fire requirements for the whole building.</p>",
    "<blockquote><p>This article refers to NCC 2022 Volume One, which applies in Queensland until NCC 2025 commences on 1 May 2027.</p></blockquote>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>The NCC measures a fire compartment's floor area and volume within its walls, but where there is no wall, the measurement includes any area or space \"which has a use which contributes to the fire load\". An open-sided awning used for loading, staging pallets or storage can therefore be counted. The building certifier decides how the definition applies to your design, so raise it at concept stage.</p>",
    "<h2>Why it matters: the limits are tight</h2>",
    "<p>NCC Table C3D3 limits a fire compartment in a Type C warehouse (Type C is the construction type that applies to most single-storey warehouses) to 2,000 m2 of floor area and 12,000 m3 of volume. Exceed either and the building needs fire walls, a higher type of construction, the large isolated building provisions or a performance solution. See fire walls and fire compartments.</p>",
    "<p>Tall warehouses reach the volume limit well before the floor area limit. Buildings are often designed close to the limit to maximise lettable area, so a small addition matters.</p>",
    "<p>Worked example: a building 60 m long with an 8 m deep awning along one side at an average height of 6 m. If the awning is counted, it adds 60 x 8 = 480 m2 of floor area and 480 x 6 = 2,880 m3 of volume. That is almost a quarter of the entire 12,000 m3 allowance for a Type C compartment.</p>",
    "<p>The same 2,000 m2 and 12,000 m3 figures are also the threshold in clause E1D13 above which an occupancy of excessive hazard needs sprinklers. See <a href=\"/insights/warehouse-fire-services-hydrants-sprinklers\">fire services</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>What the NCC definitions say</h2>",
    "<p>The NCC's defined terms (Schedule 1) include:</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Floor area</strong>, in relation to a fire compartment: the total area of all floors within the fire compartment measured within the finished internal surfaces of the bounding construction, \"and if there is no bounding construction, includes an area which has a use which contributes to the fire load\".</li><li><strong>Volume</strong>, in relation to a fire compartment: the total space measured within the fire-resisting walls (or, where a wall is not fire-resisting, over the enclosing wall), from the lowest floor to the underside of the roof covering, and \"if there is no enclosing wall, includes any space within the fire compartment that has a use which contributes to the fire load\".</li></ul>",
    "<p>So the test is not whether the awning has walls. It is whether the space beneath it has a use that contributes to the fire load, meaning combustible material that would feed a fire.</p>",
    "<p>We have not found published Australian Building Codes Board guidance that deals specifically with warehouse awnings. The interpretation sits with the building certifier on each project, and certifiers can differ. That is the reason to ask the question early and get the answer in writing.</p>",
    "<h2>When an awning is more likely to be counted</h2>",
    "<p>Treat these as prompts for a conversation with the certifier, not as rules:</p>",
    "<ol><li><strong>Goods are stored or staged under it.</strong> Pallets waiting for collection, packaging, bins, stillages and stock all contribute fire load.</li><li><strong>Work happens under it.</strong> Packing, assembly or processing extends the building's use into the space.</li><li><strong>It is partly or fully enclosed.</strong> Side sheeting, mesh, roller doors or plastic strip curtains make it part of the building in every practical sense.</li><li><strong>It is continuous with the main roof.</strong> A deep awning under the same roofline reads as an extension of the warehouse.</li><li><strong>It is likely to be filled or enclosed later.</strong> A tenant who sheets in the sides or stacks goods beneath changes the basis of the approval.</li></ol>",
    "<p>An awning that only shelters a doorway or a vehicle while it is being loaded, with nothing stored beneath it, is easier to argue as not contributing. It remains the certifier's decision.</p>",
    "<h2>Awnings and large isolated buildings</h2>",
    "<p>If the building relies on the large isolated building provisions (clause C3D4), it needs either open space at least 18 m wide around it (which must include fire truck access, and is only available for buildings up to 18,000 m2 and 108,000 m3), or sprinklers with a continuous 6 m wide perimeter access path for fire trucks. Under clause C3D5 the open space must not be used for storage or processing and must not be built on, apart from guard houses and service structures, such as substations and pump houses, that don't unduly impede firefighting. An awning, or goods stored under one, within that space can compromise the pathway. Check awning locations against the perimeter access before the site layout is fixed.</p>",
    "<h2>Design moves that help</h2>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Decide what the awning is for.</strong> Shelter over a door needs far less depth than all-weather staging for pallets.</li><li><strong>Count it from the start.</strong> Include the awning in the floor area and volume calculations at concept stage, and size the building to suit. It is easier to find later that you have spare capacity than to find you are over.</li><li><strong>Keep it open and keep it clear.</strong> If the compliance approach relies on the awning being open and free of storage, record that in the approval documents and the lease, so later occupants know.</li><li><strong>Watch the height.</strong> Volume is area multiplied by height. Lowering an awning reduces what it adds.</li><li><strong>Separate awnings between units.</strong> In multi-unit buildings, keep each unit's awning within that unit's fire compartment and don't run a continuous awning across the fire walls without resolving how the separation is maintained.</li><li><strong>Don't enclose it later without approval.</strong> Enclosing an awning is building work and changes the building's measured size. It needs a building approval.</li></ul>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>Do awnings count towards a warehouse's floor area under the NCC?</h3>",
    "<p>They can. For fire compartment purposes, the NCC includes unenclosed areas that have a use contributing to the fire load. Storage and staging of goods under an awning is such a use. The certifier decides for each building.</p>",
    "<h3>How is fire compartment volume calculated?</h3>",
    "<p>It is the total space within the compartment's walls, from the lowest floor to the underside of the roof covering. Where there is no enclosing wall, spaces with a use that contributes to the fire load are included.</p>",
    "<h3>Is fire compartment floor area the same as gross floor area?</h3>",
    "<p>No. Gross floor area is a planning term defined in the Planning Regulation 2017 and is used for planning assessment and infrastructure charges. It excludes areas such as parking, loading and vehicle manoeuvring. The NCC's floor area is a building term with a different definition. An awning can be treated differently under each.</p>",
    "<h3>Can I enclose an awning later?</h3>",
    "<p>Only with building approval. Enclosing it adds to the building's floor area and volume and may change fire, structural and planning compliance.</p>",
    "<h3>Does an awning need its own approval?</h3>",
    "<p>An awning built with the warehouse is part of the building approval. Adding one later is building work and needs approval, and it may need planning assessment if it changes site cover or setbacks.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>If your brief includes loading canopies or full-width awnings, raise fire compartment size at concept stage. Call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au, or read about <a href=\"/process\">our process</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>National Construction Code 2022, Volume One: Schedule 1 definitions of fire compartment, floor area and volume; clause C3D3 and Table C3D3; clauses C3D4 and C3D5; clause E1D13, ncc.abcb.gov.au</li><li>Planning Regulation 2017 (Qld), Schedule 24, definition of gross floor area</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information based on NCC 2022 Volume One, current as at September 2026. It is not design or certification advice. How floor area and volume are measured for your building, including the treatment of awnings, must be confirmed by your building certifier.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "warehouse-retaining-walls-types": [
    "<p>A warehouse needs a large, almost flat platform for the slab, plus hardstand graded gently for drainage. Very few industrial lots in South East Queensland arrive that way. Creating the platform means cutting into the high side, filling the low side, or both, and wherever the level change cannot be sloped out (battered) within the site, a retaining wall holds it.</p>",
    "<p>On an industrial site a retaining wall is a structure. Trucks drive next to it, buildings sit near it and stormwater builds up behind it. This article covers when a wall needs approval, what the design has to address, and how the common wall types compare.</p>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>Any retaining wall that carries load from vehicles, buildings or stored materials, retains more than 1 m, or is within 1.5 m of a building or another retaining wall needs building approval in Queensland and, in practice, engineering design. The right wall type depends on height, what is loading it, whether it is in cut or fill, how close it is to the boundary, and whether vehicles can hit it. On most industrial sites the choice comes down to concrete sleeper walls for general yard retaining and reinforced concrete or core-filled block walls where loads are heavy or space is tight.</p>",
    "<h2>When a retaining wall needs approval in Queensland</h2>",
    "<p>Under Schedule 1 of the Building Regulation 2021, a retaining wall does not need building approval only if all of the following apply:</p>",
    "<ul><li>There is no surcharge loading over the zone of influence of the wall</li><li>The total height of the wall, and of the fill or cut it retains, is no more than 1 m above the wall's natural ground surface</li><li>The wall is no closer than 1.5 m to a building or another retaining wall</li><li>It does not form part of a pool fence</li></ul>",
    "<p>\"Surcharge\" means load on the ground behind the wall, such as vehicles, a building, a stockpile or sloping ground. The \"zone of influence\" is the wedge of soil behind the wall that affects its stability. On an industrial site, almost every wall has hardstand, a building or a driveway behind it or above it, so almost every wall has a surcharge and needs approval, whatever its height. Walls that do not need approval must still comply with the building code.</p>",
    "<p>Planning approval can also be triggered by the earthworks themselves. In Brisbane, for example, planning approval is generally not needed for filling or excavation of less than 1 m, but may be needed above that, or where overlays such as the flood or waterway corridor overlays apply. Other councils have their own thresholds.</p>",
    "<h2>What the engineer has to design for</h2>",
    "<p>Retaining walls are designed by a structural or geotechnical engineer to AS 4678 (Earth-retaining structures), using soil parameters from the geotechnical report. The design addresses:</p>",
    "<ol><li><strong>Height and soil type</strong>, which set the basic earth pressure</li><li><strong>Surcharge loads</strong> from truck wheels, forklifts, containers, stockpiles and nearby footings</li><li><strong>Drainage.</strong> Water pressure behind a wall is a leading cause of failure. Walls need a free-draining granular layer behind them and a slotted drain at the base that discharges to the stormwater system, not onto the neighbour</li><li><strong>Overall stability</strong> of the slope the wall sits in, particularly walls built on or supporting fill</li><li><strong>Durability</strong> for the design life, including corrosion protection of steel posts</li><li><strong>Vehicle impact and edge protection.</strong> Where vehicles operate at the top of a wall, barriers or kerbs and a fall-protection fence are generally needed</li></ol>",
    "<h2>Boundaries and neighbours</h2>",
    "<p>In Queensland a retaining wall is not a dividing fence. The Neighbourhood Disputes (Dividing Fences and Trees) Act 2011, which shares fence costs between neighbours, does not treat a retaining wall as a fence, so its cost-sharing rules don't apply. The Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal's guidance is that retaining walls are not normally a joint responsibility, because a wall usually benefits one neighbour more than the other. In practice that is usually the owner whose cut or fill the wall supports.</p>",
    "<p>Keep the whole wall, including its footing and drainage, inside your boundary. Check for easements and underground services along the alignment. If a neighbour's existing wall or building is close, the engineer must consider the effect of your excavation on it.</p>",
    "<h2>Common wall types compared</h2>",
    "<h3>Concrete sleepers and steel posts</h3>",
    "<p>Galvanised steel posts are concreted into bored footings and precast reinforced concrete sleepers slide between them. This is the most common yard retaining wall on SEQ industrial sites.</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Advantages:</strong> fast to build, economical, suits cut and fill, damaged sleepers can be replaced individually, built from the low side so it works close to a boundary</li><li><strong>Disadvantages:</strong> post size and footing depth increase quickly with height and surcharge, functional rather than attractive, steel posts need adequate corrosion protection</li></ul>",
    "<h3>Reinforced core-filled blockwork</h3>",
    "<p>Hollow concrete blocks are laid on a reinforced concrete footing, reinforced with steel bars and filled with concrete. The Concrete Masonry Association of Australia publishes design guidance for these walls.</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Advantages:</strong> neat finish that can be rendered or painted, handles higher surcharge, slim profile</li><li><strong>Disadvantages:</strong> needs a wide footing that must fit within the boundary, slower to build, needs waterproofing and drainage behind it, costly to repair neatly after impact</li></ul>",
    "<h3>Reinforced concrete walls</h3>",
    "<p>Walls cast in place, precast, or formed with permanent formwork systems (hollow PVC or fibre-cement panels that are reinforced and filled with concrete). Tilt panels forming part of the building can also be designed to retain soil.</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Advantages:</strong> the strongest option, suited to loading docks, high walls and heavy truck surcharge, long life</li><li><strong>Disadvantages:</strong> highest cost, more excavation and formwork, longer programme. Where the building wall retains soil, waterproofing and drainage become critical</li></ul>",
    "<h3>Segmental block walls</h3>",
    "<p>Interlocking dry-stacked concrete blocks. Low walls work by their own weight. Taller walls are built as reinforced soil structures, with layers of geogrid extending back into compacted fill.</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Advantages:</strong> attractive, tolerant of minor movement, no concrete footing for many systems</li><li><strong>Disadvantages:</strong> the geogrid zone behind a taller wall extends well back into the retained fill, often a large proportion of the wall's height, and cannot later be excavated for services or footings. This suits fill sites but not cuts near a boundary</li></ul>",
    "<h3>Boulder (rock) walls</h3>",
    "<p>Large rocks stacked at a batter. Brisbane City Council has a standard drawing for this wall type.</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Advantages:</strong> natural appearance, free draining, suits landscaped edges and batters</li><li><strong>Disadvantages:</strong> takes up more width than a vertical wall, imprecise, not suited to tight hardstand edges or to carrying vehicle loads near the top</li></ul>",
    "<h3>Gabions</h3>",
    "<p>Wire baskets filled with rock. Free draining and tolerant of movement, but wide, and the wire has a finite life. Occasionally used on drainage lines and creek edges.</p>",
    "<h3>Timber sleepers</h3>",
    "<p>Not recommended for industrial sites. Timber has a limited life in the ground and is not suited to vehicle surcharge.</p>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Wall type</th><th scope=\"col\">Typical industrial use</th><th scope=\"col\">Vehicle surcharge</th><th scope=\"col\">Footprint</th><th scope=\"col\">Finish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Concrete sleeper and steel post</td><td>General yard and boundary walls</td><td>Moderate, with engineering</td><td>Narrow</td><td>Functional</td></tr><tr><td>Core-filled blockwork</td><td>Street frontages, near buildings</td><td>Good</td><td>Wide footing</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Reinforced concrete</td><td>Docks, high walls, heavy loads</td><td>Best</td><td>Moderate</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Segmental block</td><td>Landscaped frontages, fill sites</td><td>Moderate, with geogrid</td><td>Wide (geogrid zone)</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Boulder</td><td>Batters and landscaped edges</td><td>Poor</td><td>Wide</td><td>Natural</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<h2>Design tips for industrial sites</h2>",
    "<ul><li>Set the site levels, stormwater and retaining together. A small change in slab level can remove or add a whole wall</li><li>Where there is room, a batter is cheaper than a wall and needs no structure to maintain</li><li>Keep truck paths and container stacking away from the top of walls unless the wall is designed for them</li><li>Two lower walls in a terrace are not automatically simpler. If they are close together the upper wall loads the lower one and both need engineering</li><li>Get the drainage right and give it somewhere legal to discharge</li></ul>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>Do I need approval for a retaining wall in Queensland?</h3>",
    "<p>Yes, unless the wall retains no more than 1 m, has no surcharge loading, is at least 1.5 m from any building or other retaining wall, and is not part of a pool fence. On industrial sites most walls need approval.</p>",
    "<h3>What is the best retaining wall for an industrial site?</h3>",
    "<p>There isn't one answer. Concrete sleeper walls are the most common and economical for general retaining. Reinforced concrete or core-filled blockwork suits heavy loads, docks and tight spaces.</p>",
    "<h3>Who pays for a retaining wall on a boundary?</h3>",
    "<p>Retaining walls are not treated as fences under Queensland's dividing fences law. They are not normally a joint responsibility, because a wall usually benefits one neighbour more than the other, typically the owner whose cut or fill it supports. Get legal advice for a specific wall.</p>",
    "<h3>How high can a retaining wall be?</h3>",
    "<p>There is no fixed maximum. AS 4678 covers the design of walls well above the heights used on typical industrial sites. Cost rises steeply with height, so site levels are usually designed to keep walls as low as practical.</p>",
    "<h3>Can a truck park next to a retaining wall?</h3>",
    "<p>Only if the wall was designed for that load. Vehicle loads near the top of a wall add significant pressure, which is why the engineer needs to know where vehicles will travel.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>We design site levels, retaining, hardstand and stormwater as one package with the building. Call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au. See also industrial hardstand.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>Building Regulation 2021 (Qld), Schedule 1 (prescribed accepted development: retaining walls)</li><li>Brisbane City Council: retaining walls, and filling and excavation guidance, brisbane.qld.gov.au</li><li>AS 4678 Earth-retaining structures</li><li>Concrete Masonry Association of Australia, retaining wall design manuals (reinforced cantilever, reinforced soil and gravity walls)</li><li>Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal and Queensland Government guidance on dividing fences and retaining walls</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information, current as at September 2026. It is not design or legal advice. Retaining walls must be designed by a suitably qualified engineer, and approval requirements confirmed with your building certifier and council.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "warehouse-fire-services-hydrants-sprinklers": [
    "<p>Fire services are where a warehouse design meets real infrastructure cost: hydrants, hose reels, sprinklers, water tanks and pumps. Owners often find out about them late, after the floor area and height are fixed, when the hydraulic engineer asks where the tank and pump room will go.</p>",
    "<p>The triggers are set out in the National Construction Code (NCC) and they are specific. This article explains each one in plain terms.</p>",
    "<blockquote><p>This article refers to NCC 2022 Volume One, which applies in Queensland until NCC 2025 commences on 1 May 2027.</p></blockquote>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>Almost every warehouse over 500 m2 needs a fire hydrant system and fire hose reels. Sprinklers are not automatically required, but they are triggered by combustible goods stored higher than 4 m in larger fire compartments, by using the large isolated building provisions, and sometimes by tenants and insurers. Whether the town water main can supply the required flow decides whether the site needs its own tanks and pumps.</p>",
    "<h2>The triggers at a glance</h2>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Fire service</th><th scope=\"col\">NCC trigger (Class 7b and 8 buildings)</th><th scope=\"col\">NCC clause</th><th scope=\"col\">Standard</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Portable fire extinguishers</td><td>Required to suit the hazards in the building</td><td>E1D14</td><td>AS 2444</td></tr><tr><td>Fire hose reels</td><td>Where internal hydrants are installed; otherwise any fire compartment over 500 m2</td><td>E1D3</td><td>AS 2441</td></tr><tr><td>Fire hydrants</td><td>Total floor area over 500 m2, where a fire brigade station is within 50 km by road</td><td>E1D2</td><td>AS 2419.1</td></tr><tr><td>Sprinklers: excessive hazard</td><td>Fire compartment over 2,000 m2 or 12,000 m3 in an occupancy of excessive hazard</td><td>E1D13</td><td>AS 2118.1 (NCC Specification 17)</td></tr><tr><td>Sprinklers: large isolated building</td><td>Required for some large isolated building pathways</td><td>C3D4</td><td>AS 2118.1 (NCC Specification 17)</td></tr><tr><td>Smoke hazard management</td><td>Large isolated buildings</td><td>E2D10</td><td>Various</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency lighting and exit signs</td><td>Based on floor area and layout</td><td>Part E4</td><td>AS/NZS 2293.1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<h2>Fire hydrants</h2>",
    "<p>A hydrant system provides water for the fire brigade. Under clause E1D2 it is required where the building's total floor area exceeds 500 m2 and a fire brigade station equipped to use hydrants is within 50 km by road. That captures nearly every warehouse in South East Queensland.</p>",
    "<p>Hydrants can be external (pillar hydrants in the yard, or in some cases street hydrants) or internal, depending on the size and layout of the building and whether all parts of the floor can be reached by hose from outside. Where a booster assembly is required, it is located near the site entry so the fire brigade can connect a pump truck and boost the system.</p>",
    "<p>The critical question is water supply. AS 2419.1 requires the system to deliver a set flow and pressure. The hydraulic engineer obtains a flow and pressure test of the town water main from the water utility. If the main can't deliver, the site needs on-site water storage and fire pumps. A tank and pump set occupies real space, needs power and truck access, and must be allowed for in the site layout. The Queensland Fire Department publishes position statements on hydrant and water supply requirements that designers follow.</p>",
    "<h2>Fire hose reels</h2>",
    "<p>Hose reels are for use by occupants in the early stage of a fire. Under clause E1D3 they are required throughout a building that has internal hydrants, and otherwise in any fire compartment over 500 m2. Office (Class 5) buildings are among those exempt, but Class 7b warehouses and Class 8 factories are not.</p>",
    "<p>Hose reels must be located so that the hose reaches every part of the floor, which is a practical challenge in a wide warehouse once racking is installed. The racking layout and the hose reel layout should be designed together.</p>",
    "<h2>Sprinklers</h2>",
    "<p>Sprinklers are not required in every warehouse. They enter a design in four ways.</p>",
    "<h3>1. Storage that makes the building an \"occupancy of excessive hazard\"</h3>",
    "<p>Under clause E1D13, sprinklers are required in a fire compartment larger than 2,000 m2 or 12,000 m3 if the building is an occupancy of excessive hazard. That term covers:</p>",
    "<ul><li>Listed hazardous processes and storage, such as foam plastics, fireworks, aircraft hangars and flammable fibrous materials</li><li><strong>Combustible goods with a total volume of more than 1,000 m3, stored higher than 4 m.</strong> The NCC's list includes aerosols, carpets and clothing, electrical appliances, fibreboard and plywood, combustible cartons whatever their contents, furniture, paper, textiles, timber, plastics, rubber, and anything packed in foamed plastic</li></ul>",
    "<p>This matters to almost every racked warehouse. One thousand cubic metres is a modest amount of stock for a racked warehouse, and storage above 4 m is normal in pallet racking. If your operation, or a future tenant's, will store more than 1,000 m3 of ordinary packaged goods above 4 m, the deemed-to-satisfy choices are to keep each compartment within 2,000 m2 and 12,000 m3, or to sprinkler it.</p>",
    "<p>The thresholds match the Type C compartment limits in Table C3D3, so a Type C building divided into complying compartments stays below this trigger. See fire walls and fire compartments.</p>",
    "<h3>2. Large isolated buildings</h3>",
    "<p>A warehouse that exceeds the normal compartment limits under the large isolated building provisions (clause C3D4) needs sprinklers if it is over 18,000 m2 or 108,000 m3, or if it doesn't have an 18 m wide open space around it. The open-space option is limited to Class 7 or 8 buildings of no more than 2 storeys and must include fire truck access.</p>",
    "<h3>3. Height and other uses</h3>",
    "<p>Buildings with an effective height over 25 m (roughly, the height of the top floor above the lowest ground-level floor) need sprinklers under clause E1D5, as do some other building classes. This rarely applies to a conventional warehouse.</p>",
    "<h3>4. Tenant and insurer requirements</h3>",
    "<p>Major logistics tenants and some insurers require sprinklers, often to a higher specification than the NCC minimum. If the building is intended for that market, find out before design.</p>",
    "<h3>What a sprinkler system involves</h3>",
    "<ul><li>A water supply sized for the storage arrangement, which generally means dedicated tanks and pumps</li><li>Pipework and heads at roof level, and sometimes within the racking</li><li>A design based on what is stored, how high and in what packaging. Changing the storage later can mean upgrading the system</li><li>Clearance between the top of stored goods and the sprinkler heads</li><li>A valve room, an alarm connection and fire brigade access</li></ul>",
    "<h2>Ongoing maintenance is a legal obligation</h2>",
    "<p>In Queensland, fire hydrants, hose reels, sprinklers, fire detection, emergency lighting and similar systems are \"prescribed fire safety installations\". Under the Building Fire Safety Regulation 2008 and Queensland Development Code MP 6.1, the occupier must have them maintained by appropriately qualified people in accordance with AS 1851, keep maintenance records, and prepare an occupier statement each year.</p>",
    "<p>AS 1851 sets routine service intervals. For sprinkler systems these include monthly, six-monthly, yearly and five-yearly activities, with further testing as systems age. For tanks, pumps and hydrants there are regular tests and periodic major services. Allow for this in the building's outgoings. It is one reason some owners prefer fire walls and smaller compartments where that gives a complying building without sprinklers.</p>",
    "<h2>How the fire brigade is involved in approval</h2>",
    "<p>Hydrants, sprinklers, fire detection and alarm systems, smoke exhaust and vehicular access for large isolated buildings are \"special fire services\" under the Planning Regulation 2017. A building application that includes them, or that includes a fire safety performance solution, must be referred to Queensland Fire and Rescue (part of the Queensland Fire Department) for advice. The fire service can also inspect and test the installed systems before the certificate of occupancy is issued. See the certification pathway.</p>",
    "<h2>Why these decisions belong at concept stage</h2>",
    "<ul><li>Floor area, height and storage method together decide whether sprinklers are needed</li><li>A tank and pump set needs space, access and power</li><li>Hose reel and hydrant coverage depends on the building's shape and the racking layout</li><li>Awnings can add to floor area and volume. See <a href=\"/insights/warehouse-awnings-fire-compartment-volume\">awnings and fire volume</a></li><li>The fire service referral takes time and must be in the programme</li></ul>",
    "<p>The cheapest fire strategy is the one decided together with the floor area and height.</p>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>When does a warehouse need sprinklers?</h3>",
    "<p>Under the NCC, mainly when a fire compartment over 2,000 m2 or 12,000 m3 holds more than 1,000 m3 of combustible goods stored above 4 m, or contains a listed hazardous process, or when the building relies on the large isolated building provisions. Tenants and insurers may also require them.</p>",
    "<h3>Does every warehouse need fire hydrants?</h3>",
    "<p>Every warehouse with a total floor area over 500 m2, where a fire station is within 50 km by road, needs a hydrant system.</p>",
    "<h3>Do I need a fire water tank?</h3>",
    "<p>For hydrants, only if the town water main can't supply the required flow and pressure. A flow and pressure test early in design answers that. Sprinkler systems for storage warehouses generally need their own tanks and pumps.</p>",
    "<h3>Are fire hose reels required in a warehouse?</h3>",
    "<p>Yes, in any fire compartment over 500 m2, and throughout any building with internal hydrants.</p>",
    "<h3>Who maintains the fire systems after handover?</h3>",
    "<p>The occupier is responsible under the Building Fire Safety Regulation 2008. Leases usually set out how the cost is shared between owner and tenant.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>Before you fix the height, awning depth and floor area, ask for a fire services check. Call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au, or read about <a href=\"/process\">our process</a>.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>National Construction Code 2022, Volume One: clauses E1D2, E1D3, E1D4, E1D5, E1D13 and E1D14; clause C3D4; clause E2D10; Part E4; Table C3D3, ncc.abcb.gov.au</li><li>AS 2419.1 Fire hydrant installations; AS 2441 Installation of fire hose reels; AS 2118.1 Automatic fire sprinkler systems; AS 1851 Routine service of fire protection systems and equipment</li><li>Queensland Fire Department, referral agency advice guidelines and position statements, fire.qld.gov.au</li><li>Building Fire Safety Regulation 2008 (Qld); Queensland Development Code MP 6.1</li><li>Planning Regulation 2017 (Qld), Schedule 9 Part 3 Division 3 and Schedule 19</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information based on NCC 2022 Volume One, current as at September 2026. It is not design or certification advice. Fire services for your building must be designed by a qualified fire services engineer and confirmed by your building certifier.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "warehouse-insulation-condensation-section-j": [
    "<p>South East Queensland is warm and humid for much of the year. For a warehouse that raises three related questions: how hot will it get inside, will the roof drip, and what does the National Construction Code (NCC) require for energy efficiency? They are connected, and fixing one without thinking about the others can make things worse.</p>",
    "<blockquote><p>This article refers to NCC 2022 Volume One, which applies in Queensland until NCC 2025 commences on 1 May 2027.</p></blockquote>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>The NCC's energy efficiency provisions (Section J) apply to warehouses, but the insulation requirements apply to air-conditioned space. In a typical warehouse that means the office, including the wall and ceiling between the office and the warehouse. The unconditioned warehouse is generally not required to be insulated, but still has lighting, metering and solar-ready requirements. Condensation under metal roofs is not regulated for warehouses by the NCC's condensation provisions, so it has to be dealt with through good design: a roof blanket with a vapour barrier, and ventilation.</p>",
    "<h2>Which climate zone is SEQ?</h2>",
    "<p>The NCC divides Australia into eight climate zones. Brisbane and the coastal parts of South East Queensland are in climate zone 2: warm humid summers and mild winters. Toowoomba and the Darling Downs are in zone 5. The zone sets the insulation and glazing requirements.</p>",
    "<h2>How Section J applies to a warehouse</h2>",
    "<p>Section J applies to Class 5 offices, Class 7b warehouses and Class 8 factories. How much it affects the building depends on two defined terms:</p>",
    "<ul><li>A <strong>conditioned space</strong> is a space likely to have its temperature controlled by air-conditioning.</li><li>The <strong>envelope</strong> is the parts of the building fabric that separate a conditioned space from the outside, or from a non-conditioned space. The NCC's examples of a non-conditioned space include a warehouse.</li></ul>",
    "<p>The building fabric provisions (Part J4: roof, walls, glazing and floor) apply to the envelope. The practical result:</p>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part of the building</th><th scope=\"col\">How Section J typically applies</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Air-conditioned office, showroom or lunchroom</td><td>Full fabric requirements: roof and ceiling insulation, wall insulation, glazing performance, sealing</td></tr><tr><td>Wall and ceiling between the office and the warehouse</td><td>Part of the envelope, so it needs insulation. This is frequently missed</td></tr><tr><td>Unconditioned warehouse</td><td>Generally no fabric insulation requirement, because the NCC's insulation rules apply only to the fabric around air-conditioned space. Lighting, metering and solar-ready provisions still apply</td></tr><tr><td>Air-conditioned or temperature-controlled warehouse</td><td>The whole warehouse becomes conditioned space and the fabric provisions apply to all of it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<p>Key NCC 2022 requirements worth knowing:</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Roof insulation over conditioned space (J4D4).</strong> In climate zone 2, a total R-value of R3.7 for downward heat flow.</li><li><strong>Roof colour (J4D4).</strong> The upper surface of a roof over conditioned space must have a solar absorptance of no more than 0.45 to use the deemed-to-satisfy pathway. Colours with a published solar absorptance of 0.45 or less comply. That covers the light colours and some mid-tones, so check the manufacturer's figure. Dark colours do not. BlueScope advises that unpainted ZINCALUME® steel should be treated as having a solar absorptance of 0.50 to 0.60, which is above the limit.</li><li><strong>Walls and glazing (J4D6).</strong> The combined wall and glazing performance is assessed together, so large areas of unshaded office glazing need higher-performance glass or external shading.</li><li><strong>Sealing (J5).</strong> A loading dock entrance leading to a conditioned space must have a rapid roller door or similar.</li><li><strong>Lighting (J7).</strong> Maximum lighting power density applies throughout, including the warehouse. The allowance for storage areas is much lower than for offices, which efficient LED lighting generally meets.</li><li><strong>Energy metering (J9D3).</strong> Buildings over 500 m2 need energy meters that record time-of-use consumption. Buildings over 2,500 m2 need separate metering of major energy uses.</li><li><strong>Electric vehicle charging (J9D4).</strong> Where a car park serving the building has 10 or more spaces on a storey, it needs dedicated electrical distribution boards sized for future EV charging. For warehouses and factories the boards are sized to serve 20% of the spaces.</li><li><strong>Solar-ready (J9D5).</strong> The main switchboard must have capacity and space for a solar photovoltaic system and battery, and at least 20% of the roof area must be left clear for solar panels, with some exemptions. See solar on warehouse roofs.</li></ul>",
    "<p>A Section J report, usually by an energy efficiency consultant, is normally required by the certifier as part of the building approval documents. Any building can instead use a performance-based assessment (energy modelling against a reference building), which is most often done for large or unusual buildings.</p>",
    "<h2>Why warehouse roofs drip</h2>",
    "<p>Condensation forms when warm, moist air meets a surface colder than its dew point. On clear nights a metal roof radiates heat to the sky and becomes colder than the air around it. Humid air inside the building rises, meets the cold underside of the sheeting and condenses. The water drips on stock and floors, and over time it corrodes the sheeting, purlins and fixings.</p>",
    "<p>Concrete tilt panels can also sweat in humid, still conditions, and air-conditioned offices can get condensation inside walls and ceilings where warm humid air reaches a cooled surface.</p>",
    "<p><strong>The NCC does not regulate this for warehouses.</strong> Part F8 (Condensation management) of NCC 2022 Volume One applies only to sole-occupancy units in Class 2 buildings (apartments) and Class 4 parts. It does not apply to Class 5, 7 or 8 buildings. Managing condensation in a warehouse is therefore a matter of good design and protecting the building's durability, not code compliance. Useful references are the Australian Building Codes Board's Condensation in Buildings handbook and the roofing manufacturers' technical guidance.</p>",
    "<h2>How to control heat and condensation</h2>",
    "<p><strong>Roof insulation blanket.</strong> The standard approach is a glasswool blanket faced with reflective foil, laid over safety mesh directly beneath the roof sheeting. The blanket keeps the underside surface (the foil) warmer than the roof sheet, and the foil acts as a vapour barrier so far less humid air reaches the cold metal. It also cuts radiant heat in summer and deadens rain noise. It works best when the foil is continuous, with joints lapped and taped. BlueScope's technical guidance warns that a blanket which gets wet can hold moisture against the sheeting and accelerate corrosion, so installation quality matters.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Roof colour.</strong> A light roof reflects far more heat. BlueScope's guidance is that a light-coloured roof surface can be up to 35 degrees C cooler than a dark one in summer. A light colour is the cheapest thermal measure on any building.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Ventilation.</strong> Ridge vents, rotary vents, wall louvres and open doors move hot, humid air out. Ventilation reduces both heat build-up and condensation.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Translucent roof sheeting.</strong> Skylights save lighting energy but admit heat, and they are a fall hazard for anyone on the roof. Safety mesh beneath them is essential. Use them where daylight is wanted, not by default.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Offices.</strong> Most comfort complaints in industrial buildings come from the office. Treat it as a separate, properly insulated and sealed box inside or alongside the warehouse: insulated ceiling, insulated wall to the warehouse, shaded glazing, and lining and insulation to any concrete panel walls.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Relevant standards.</strong> Insulation materials are specified to AS/NZS 4859.1, and pliable membranes (sarking and vapour barriers) to AS 4200.1, installed to AS 4200.2.</p>",
    "<h2>How the wall system affects it</h2>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Full concrete tilt panel</strong>: high thermal mass, so it heats slowly and cools slowly. West-facing panels radiate heat inward well into the evening. Offices behind them need insulated linings.</li><li><strong>Dado with cladding above</strong>: the metal-clad portion behaves like a steel-clad wall, and the junction needs careful sealing.</li><li><strong>Steel frame and metal cladding</strong>: heats and cools quickly. Foil-faced blanket in the walls makes a large difference to comfort.</li></ul>",
    "<p>See wall systems compared.</p>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>Does a warehouse have to be insulated under the NCC?</h3>",
    "<p>An unconditioned warehouse generally doesn't, because the NCC's fabric insulation requirements apply to the envelope of conditioned space. The air-conditioned office does, including its walls and ceiling to the warehouse. Roof insulation in the warehouse is still strongly recommended for heat and condensation.</p>",
    "<h3>What is Section J?</h3>",
    "<p>Section J of NCC Volume One sets energy efficiency requirements for commercial buildings, covering building fabric, sealing, air-conditioning, lighting, hot water, metering and provision for solar and EV charging.</p>",
    "<h3>How do I stop condensation under a metal warehouse roof?</h3>",
    "<p>Install a foil-faced insulation blanket directly under the roof sheeting with the foil joints sealed, and ventilate the building. Without the blanket, a metal roof in a humid climate is likely to drip in the right conditions.</p>",
    "<h3>What roof colour should a warehouse have?</h3>",
    "<p>A light colour. It lowers internal temperatures, and over air-conditioned space the NCC deemed-to-satisfy provisions require a solar absorptance of no more than 0.45.</p>",
    "<h3>Do new warehouses need to be solar-ready?</h3>",
    "<p>Yes. Under NCC 2022 clause J9D5, new buildings need switchboard provision for solar and batteries and, subject to exemptions, 20% of the roof area kept clear for panels.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>Tell us which areas will be air-conditioned and what you will store, and we will design the envelope to suit. Call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>National Construction Code 2022, Volume One: Section J (Parts J1, J4, J5, J7 and J9); Part F8; Schedule 1 definitions of conditioned space and envelope, ncc.abcb.gov.au</li><li>Business Queensland, NCC climate zones in Queensland</li><li>Australian Building Codes Board, Condensation in Buildings handbook</li><li>BlueScope, Corrosion Technical Bulletin CTB-11 (Roof condensation and insulation) and Technical Bulletin TB-39 (Thermal performance of roofing materials)</li><li>AS/NZS 4859.1 Thermal insulation materials for buildings; AS 4200.1 and AS 4200.2 Pliable building membranes and underlays</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information based on NCC 2022 Volume One, current as at September 2026. It is not an energy efficiency assessment or specification. Section J compliance for your building must be confirmed by your energy efficiency consultant and building certifier.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
  "asbestos-hazardous-materials-industrial-upgrades": [
    "<p>Not every project is a new building on a clear site. Owners and tenants also extend older warehouses, refit offices, widen doors, replace roofs and carry out make-good works at the end of a lease. In older industrial buildings, asbestos and other hazardous materials can sit undisturbed for decades until someone cuts, drills or demolishes.</p>",
    "<p>This article explains what Queensland law requires and how to build it into a project. It is not a guide to handling asbestos. That work belongs to trained and licensed people.</p>",
    "<h2>The short answer</h2>",
    "<p>If the building was constructed before 1990, assume it contains asbestos until a competent person shows otherwise. Queensland law requires an asbestos register for most workplaces, and requires asbestos that is likely to be disturbed to be identified and, so far as is reasonably practicable, removed before refurbishment or demolition starts. Removal of friable asbestos, or more than 10 m2 of non-friable asbestos, must be done by a licensed removalist, followed by an independent clearance inspection.</p>",
    "<h2>Where asbestos is found in industrial buildings</h2>",
    "<p>Asbestos was widely used in building products until the late 1980s, and all forms were banned nationally from 31 December 2003. In older factories and warehouses it is commonly found in:</p>",
    "<ul><li>Corrugated asbestos cement roof and wall sheeting, gutters and downpipes</li><li>Flat asbestos cement sheet in offices, amenities, eaves and partitions</li><li>Vinyl floor tiles and their adhesive</li><li>Backing boards in old electrical switchboards</li><li>Lagging on pipes, boilers and ovens</li><li>Gaskets, seals and friction materials in plant</li><li>Fire doors</li><li>Mastics, sealants and window putty</li></ul>",
    "<p><strong>Friable</strong> asbestos can be crumbled to powder by hand, such as lagging and sprayed insulation. It is the higher risk. <strong>Non-friable</strong> (bonded) asbestos, such as cement sheeting, is bound in a matrix and is lower risk while intact, but weathered, broken or fire-damaged sheeting can release fibres.</p>",
    "<p>Asbestos cement roofs carry a second hazard. They are brittle, will not support a person's weight and are a recognised cause of fatal falls. Queensland's work health and safety regulation also prohibits using high-pressure water or compressed air on asbestos, so an asbestos cement roof must never be pressure cleaned.</p>",
    "<h2>What Queensland law requires</h2>",
    "<p>The duties are in Chapter 8 of the Work Health and Safety Regulation 2011 (Qld) and the two asbestos codes of practice.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Asbestos register and management plan.</strong> The person with management or control of a workplace must keep an asbestos register, prepared by a competent person. A register is not required only where the building was constructed after 31 December 1989 and no asbestos has been identified or is likely to be present. Where asbestos is present or assumed, a written asbestos management plan is also required. Both must be kept current and be available to workers and contractors.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Before refurbishment or demolition.</strong> The register must be reviewed before the work and given to the person doing it. Asbestos likely to be disturbed must be identified and, so far as is reasonably practicable, removed before the work starts. A standard register often covers only accessible areas, so planned works usually need a more intrusive survey of the specific areas affected, such as inside wall cavities, above ceilings and under floor coverings.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Licensed removal.</strong></p>",
    "<div class=\"insight-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Work</th><th scope=\"col\">Who can do it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Any amount of friable asbestos</td><td>Class A licensed asbestos removalist</td></tr><tr><td>More than 10 m2 of non-friable asbestos</td><td>Class A or Class B licensed asbestos removalist</td></tr><tr><td>Up to 10 m2 of non-friable asbestos</td><td>No licence required, but the regulation's safety requirements still apply and workers must be trained</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    "<p><strong>Notification.</strong> A licensed removalist must notify Workplace Health and Safety Queensland at least five days before licensed removal work starts.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Air monitoring.</strong> Required for friable (Class A) removal, carried out by an independent licensed asbestos assessor.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Clearance.</strong> After licensed removal, the area cannot be reoccupied until a clearance inspection has been done and a clearance certificate issued: by an independent licensed asbestos assessor for Class A work, and by an independent competent person for Class B work.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Disposal.</strong> Asbestos waste must be contained, transported and disposed of at a facility licensed to accept it.</p>",
    "<h2>Other hazardous materials</h2>",
    "<p>A hazardous materials survey before major works looks for more than asbestos:</p>",
    "<ul><li><strong>Lead paint.</strong> Common on older steelwork and timber. For house paint, the recommended maximum lead content was reduced from 50% before 1965 to 1% in 1965, 0.25% in 1992 and 0.1% in 1997. Industrial coatings on old steelwork, such as red lead primers, can contain far more. Treat old paint as lead-bearing until tested. Sanding, grinding or flame-cutting lead-painted steel creates toxic dust and fume.</li><li><strong>Synthetic mineral fibre (SMF).</strong> Glasswool and rockwool insulation. An irritant that needs controls when disturbed.</li><li><strong>PCBs.</strong> Polychlorinated biphenyls, found in old capacitors, transformers and fluorescent light fittings.</li><li><strong>Refrigerants.</strong> Older air-conditioning and cold room plant may contain ozone-depleting gases, which must be recovered by licensed technicians.</li><li><strong>Contaminated land.</strong> Former industrial uses may have contaminated the soil. Queensland's Environmental Management Register and Contaminated Land Register record land used for notifiable activities, such as fuel storage, and land known to be contaminated. A search should be part of due diligence before buying, and before any excavation.</li><li><strong>Silica dust.</strong> Cutting, grinding and drilling concrete, tilt panels, blocks and tiles produces respirable crystalline silica, which causes silicosis. Queensland has a code of practice for managing silica dust in construction, and strengthened regulations for processing crystalline silica materials, adopted nationally and in Queensland, took effect on 1 September 2024. Expect wet cutting, on-tool dust extraction and respiratory protection on any compliant site.</li></ul>",
    "<h2>A sensible process for an upgrade</h2>",
    "<ol><li><strong>Check the history.</strong> Construction date, the existing asbestos register, previous removal records and the land's register status</li><li><strong>Commission a survey suited to the works</strong> before pricing demolition or strip-out</li><li><strong>Build the findings into the scope and programme</strong>, including removal, clearance and any temporary works</li><li><strong>Use licensed removalists</strong>, and an assessor who is independent of the removalist</li><li><strong>Separate the work areas</strong> and manage other trades and occupants around the removal</li><li><strong>Get the clearance certificate</strong> before the next trade starts</li><li><strong>Update the asbestos register</strong> for whatever remains in the building</li></ol>",
    "<p>The most expensive asbestos is the asbestos found halfway through a job. Work stops, the area may need decontamination, and the programme and cost are no longer in your control.</p>",
    "<h2>Leases and make-goods</h2>",
    "<p>Asbestos is a frequent cause of disputes at the end of a lease. Typical triggers are a make-good scope that requires removing fitout fixed to asbestos sheeting, a register that was never given to the tenant, or contractors starting strip-out without a survey. Owners should keep the register current and hand it over at the start of each lease. Tenants should ask for it before signing and before starting any fitout. Buyers should obtain the register and the records of past works as part of due diligence.</p>",
    "<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>",
    "<h3>How do I know if my warehouse contains asbestos?</h3>",
    "<p>You can't tell by looking. If the building dates from before 1990, assume it does until a competent person has inspected and, where needed, had samples tested by an accredited laboratory.</p>",
    "<h3>Is an asbestos register a legal requirement in Queensland?</h3>",
    "<p>Yes, for workplaces, unless the building was constructed after 31 December 1989 and no asbestos has been identified or is likely to be present.</p>",
    "<h3>Can I remove asbestos myself?</h3>",
    "<p>In a workplace, only up to 10 m2 of non-friable asbestos can be removed without a licence, and the safety requirements still apply. Anything more, or any friable asbestos, needs a licensed removalist. We recommend using a licensed removalist regardless of quantity.</p>",
    "<h3>Can solar panels be installed on an asbestos cement roof?</h3>",
    "<p>It isn't practical or safe. The sheeting is brittle, can't be walked on, and drilling it disturbs asbestos. Replace the roof first.</p>",
    "<h3>Who pays for asbestos removal in a leased building?</h3>",
    "<p>It depends on the lease. Generally the owner is responsible for the building fabric, but make-good and fitout clauses vary. Get legal advice on the specific lease.</p>",
    "<h2>Talk to Brenscot</h2>",
    "<p>If you are planning an upgrade to an older industrial building, put a hazardous materials survey on the programme before treating any demolition price as firm. Call 0480 800 077 or email enquiries@brenscot.com.au.</p>",
    "<h2>Sources</h2>",
    "<ul><li>Work Health and Safety Regulation 2011 (Qld), Chapter 8 (asbestos), including sections 425, 429, 446, 458, 466 and 473 to 475</li><li>How to manage and control asbestos in the workplace Code of Practice 2021; How to safely remove asbestos Code of Practice 2021; Demolition work Code of Practice 2021; Managing the risk of falls at workplaces Code of Practice 2021, WorkSafe Queensland</li><li>Managing respirable crystalline silica dust exposure in construction and manufacturing of construction elements Code of Practice 2022, WorkSafe Queensland</li><li>Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water, Lead in house paint</li><li>Queensland Government, Environmental Management Register and Contaminated Land Register; Environmental Protection Act 1994, Schedule 3</li></ul>",
    "<h2>General information only</h2>",
    "<p>This article is general information, current as at September 2026. It is not legal or work health and safety advice, and it is not guidance on how to handle asbestos. Do not disturb suspected asbestos. Engage a licensed asbestos assessor and licensed removalist, and obtain advice specific to your building.</p>",
  ].join("\n"),
};
